# Docker does not read .gitignore, so the artifacts ignored there are listed
# again here. Without this, a host-built binary and any local SQLite database
# would be shipped into the build context: wasted transfer at best, and a stale
# binary sitting next to the one the builder produces at worst.
#
# Patterns are anchored or recursive deliberately, not uniformly. Docker's
# patterns do not cross a / on their own, unlike git's, so a bare *.db matches
# only the context root. Where recursion is safe it is spelled with **.
#
# pulsar-indexer is the exception and must stay anchored to the root. It is the
# name of both the host build output and the source directory cmd/pulsar-indexer,
# so a recursive **/pulsar-indexer excludes the package the image is built from
# and the build fails with "stat /src/cmd/pulsar-indexer: directory not found".

# Host build output, root only: see the note above.
/pulsar-indexer
/bin/

# Coverage and profile output, safe to exclude at any depth.
**/*.out

# Local SQLite databases, including WAL and shared-memory sidecars.
**/*.db
**/*.db-shm
**/*.db-wal

# Environment files. None belongs in this directory (the tracked template and
# the developer's .env.local both live at the repository root, outside this
# context), so this is a guard against one being created here later: git would
# hide it, since the root .gitignore's .env patterns match at any depth, and it
# would otherwise reach a builder layer, which a registry cache export would
# then publish.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Not needed to compile.
README.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
